+### Reading the code identity and the citation ledger (round 53)
+
+- The C1 counter reads the running server's memory itself, so it needs Linux `/proc` and ptrace read access to its own descendants (Yama `ptrace_scope` 0 or 1; the counter starts the replay, so the server is its descendant). It does not use `/proc/<pid>/map_files`: opening it needs CAP_SYS_ADMIN or CAP_CHECKPOINT_RESTORE, which this host lacks (S508). A host library is accepted on ownership (uid 0), inode and writability alone; its dev is not compared. Stated limit: the counter hashes the archive itself and compares the mapped range, so a library swapped after the archive's member check is caught by the digest and memory legs, while a byte-identical host install of the same libraries is accepted by design.
+- The C2 counter re-derives every correction: a miss with no ledger row is `undisclosed`, and a row whose class check fails is `bad`, so a citation corrected in place makes its row stale (exit 1) until the row is superseded by a dated comment in `registry/cited-path-corrections.tsv`. A new unresolved citation needs a row before the counter can return 0.
